  "test_contract": {
    "expected_observation": "Accounts of fleas, flies and blister beetles say they arise from putrefying matter and do not describe a parental egg-to-adult cycle.",
    "disconfirming_observation": "An account of any one of those kinds describes eggs laid by the insect and development into an adult of the same kind.",
    "admissible_datasets": "A public species account that states the life cycle of a flea (Ctenocephalides felis), a fly (Musca domestica), and a blister beetle, the modern referent of cantharides. The first account in the candidate list that contains a reproduction or development section is used. Classification stubs are not accounts.",
    "inference_method": "Code egg-laying, a four-stage or holometabolous life cycle, and spontaneous-generation wording with the phrases in rules. One parental life cycle refutes the claim that these insects do not come from parents."
  },
  "interpretation": "Cantharides is read as a blister beetle (Meloidae), with Lytta vesicatoria first and the other named accounts only if that page is not a life-cycle account. 'Produce only a scolex, not a creature of their own kind' is refuted by a stated development from egg to adult of the same species. The test uses published life-cycle prose, not a new rearing experiment.",
